WEBVTT

00:00.180 --> 00:01.890
Instructor: Next up, this will be a quick one.

00:01.890 --> 00:04.170
We'll talk about the HEAD file.

00:04.170 --> 00:06.870
So, HEAD in all caps, that's the name of the file.

00:06.870 --> 00:09.690
It exists in our .git directory.

00:09.690 --> 00:11.490
So if we take a look at .git,

00:11.490 --> 00:13.410
there it is, HEAD,

00:13.410 --> 00:15.360
and it's just a plain text file

00:15.360 --> 00:18.060
and it contains a reference to a commit

00:18.060 --> 00:20.670
or to a branch reference, normally.

00:20.670 --> 00:22.920
So as we've talked about in our diagrams,

00:22.920 --> 00:25.650
HEAD typically refers to a branch pointer

00:25.650 --> 00:28.020
but we can go into detached HEAD,

00:28.020 --> 00:30.270
at which point HEAD refers to a particular commit

00:30.270 --> 00:31.680
and that's why it's detached.

00:31.680 --> 00:33.480
Detached, detached.

00:33.480 --> 00:36.300
Okay, so we can take a look at it.

00:36.300 --> 00:40.380
Currently, I'm on the master branch on this react repo.

00:40.380 --> 00:41.650
Here's the HEAD file

00:42.630 --> 00:46.110
and it says, inside of it, so I'll make it a bit larger.

00:46.110 --> 00:46.943
It just says,

00:46.943 --> 00:50.700
"Okay, take a look at whatever's in refs/heads/master"

00:50.700 --> 00:53.820
which is a commit as we saw in the prior video.

00:53.820 --> 00:56.970
So, it's a reference to a reference, right?

00:56.970 --> 00:58.830
It is pointing,

00:58.830 --> 01:00.060
if I go back, here we are,

01:00.060 --> 01:01.740
it's pointing, in that file,

01:01.740 --> 01:04.140
to the contents of the master reference,

01:04.140 --> 01:05.550
master branch reference.

01:05.550 --> 01:08.940
If I switch branches, what do we have?

01:08.940 --> 01:12.693
Let's switch to chicken, git switch chicken.

01:13.710 --> 01:17.790
Now it contains refs/heads/chicken

01:17.790 --> 01:19.560
and we can take a look at that.

01:19.560 --> 01:21.870
This is the commit it's referring to.

01:21.870 --> 01:23.280
It's not pointing to the commit though.

01:23.280 --> 01:24.690
It's pointing to the branch

01:24.690 --> 01:26.760
and the branch is referring to this commit.

01:26.760 --> 01:28.500
That's the tip of the branch.

01:28.500 --> 01:30.030
However, I could go back, right?

01:30.030 --> 01:32.370
If I pick one commit,

01:32.370 --> 01:34.650
let's just pick one of them, doesn't matter.

01:34.650 --> 01:36.090
Right here.

01:36.090 --> 01:38.460
If I copy this hash or just a portion of it

01:38.460 --> 01:41.460
and I do a git checkout with that hash,

01:41.460 --> 01:42.660
we've seen it before,

01:42.660 --> 01:44.880
I'm now in detached HEAD.

01:44.880 --> 01:47.280
If we look at the HEAD file,

01:47.280 --> 01:50.190
now it directly points to the commit.

01:50.190 --> 01:52.770
So, the branch reference has not moved.

01:52.770 --> 01:57.270
It's still pointing to that 5fb, but HEAD has.

01:57.270 --> 02:00.300
Just like in this slide here, we detach the HEAD.

02:00.300 --> 02:01.620
It refers to a commit.

02:01.620 --> 02:03.540
The branch reference is still in the same place,

02:03.540 --> 02:05.040
it's just pointing at that commit.

02:05.040 --> 02:08.010
It contains the commit inside of the file.

02:08.010 --> 02:09.930
Now, HEAD contains a commit

02:09.930 --> 02:12.990
instead of containing a reference to a branch.

02:12.990 --> 02:14.700
So now if I get out of this,

02:14.700 --> 02:16.650
let's go, this time let's go to master,

02:16.650 --> 02:18.183
git switch to master.

02:19.020 --> 02:22.590
Now, HEAD is updated to reflect that change.

02:22.590 --> 02:24.633
All right, so that's the HEAD file.
